^^Vowel harmony^^

By now you may have noticed that all Finnish words end in a vowel. Vowels are very important in Finnish, and what’s really good to recognize in the very beginning is that the language only allows certain vowels to exist together inside a word. This is called the *vowel harmony* of the language, and in Finnish it looks like this:


a o u -- can exist together
i e -- can exist with any vowels
ä ö y -- can exist together


For example, the words *kirjastakohan* ('possibly about the book') and *pöydästäköhän* ('possibly about the table') both have the exact same endings (-sta -ko -han) appended to them, but the endings in the second word change their vowels to 'ä' and ‘ö’ respectively, in order to accomodate for the vowels ‘ä’ and ‘ö’ that came before. *Pöytä* (table) only has 'ö' and 'ä' -vowels, which means the vowels 'a' 'o' 'u' are forbidden, therefore its endings can only contain ‘y’, ‘ä’ and ‘ö’. 
*Kirja* (book) on the other hand has an 'a', which means any possible endings can only contain 'a', 'o' and 'u' and never ‘y’, ‘ä’ or ‘ö’ (refer to the table above). There is no word where for example 'a' and 'ä' exist together, except in compound words:


*pöytäkirja* (lit. 'table book', more correctly 'protocol/transcript').


In a compound word the endings take the vowel of the last word’s vowel group, in this case 'a' 'o' 'u': *pöytäkirja-sta-ko-han* ('possibly about the protocol')

Note: in a word that *only* contains the wildcard letters 'i' and 'e’, such as *kieli* (language, tongue), the endings will correspond with the lower group vowels 'ä' 'ö' 'y': *kielestäköhän* (possibly about the language)


^A final note^
The vowels ‘ä’ and ‘ö’ are very often mixed up with ‘a’ and ‘o’ by beginners. We recommend you pay extra attention to these two examples, even if your native language already has this vowel distinction:

*Hyvää päivää* (Good day!) - not ‘hyvaa päivaa’
*tässä* (right here) - not ‘tässa’


Note also the long vowel ending. In a later skill we will explain why you say *hyvä päivä* (a good day) but *Hyvää päivää!* (Good day!)